{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "rls-multi-tenant",
  "domain": "security-and-safe-sql",
  "value": ["corrects-a-wrong-assumption", "multi-step-with-silent-failures"],
  "correction": "Asked for tenant isolation on Azure SQL Database, an agent writes a security policy carrying a filter predicate and presents it as finished, because every SELECT then returns only the current tenant's rows and the demonstration looks correct. Measured on 2026-08-28 against a live engine reporting EngineEdition 5 and Edition 'SQL Azure': with a filter predicate alone, a session holding tenant 1 inserted a row stamped tenant 2 and the statement succeeded, then updated one of its own rows into tenant 2 and that succeeded too, and both rows immediately vanished from the writer's view, so the application that created them could neither read them back nor delete them; recovery required disabling the policy. Adding the block predicate turned both into Msg 33504, and AFTER INSERT and AFTER UPDATE are separately required, because with only AFTER INSERT in place the tenant-move update still succeeded with no error. The second wrong belief is that SESSION_CONTEXT is a tenant boundary. It is per-connection state any caller on that connection can rewrite with no permission at all: a user holding only CONNECT plus SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE on one table reset its own tenant id and read the other tenant's rows. It also outlives the request. With the context set in one pooled call and the query issued in another, 200 concurrent requests over a five connection pool returned the wrong tenant's rows 103 times, and 91 times on a repeat, with no error raised to any caller, while setting the context and querying on one acquired connection returned 0 wrong answers in 200 and a database user per tenant with one pool each also returned 0 in 200; sp_set_session_context with @read_only = 1 is not the fix, since on a reused connection it refused the next tenant's correct attempt with Msg 15664 while the forgetful path still leaked. Bypass is narrower than usually recalled and was confirmed rather than assumed: neither the table owner nor a db_owner member reads through the policy, both returning 0 rows with no tenant set, and what they hold is the ability to disable it, which requires ALTER ANY SECURITY POLICY together with ALTER on the schema holding the policy, since either permission alone failed with Msg 33268. A policy can also be present and not enforcing in three ways that all look healthy from the application, namely STATE = OFF, a predicate that returns a row when the tenant is unset, and a second tenant-scoped table nobody added to the policy. Being wrong costs a cross-tenant write no read can find and no application statement can undo, a tenant reading another tenant's rows about half the time under ordinary production concurrency with nothing raised and nothing logged, and an isolation control that ships never having been exercised beside its vector index, because the local container refuses to hold a security policy and a vector index on the same table in either order, Msg 37579 and Msg 42244.",
    "value_probe": {
      "gate": "The answer stops a wrong-tenant write at the database instead of only hiding rows from reads, and it ties the tenant value to the same connection that runs the query so a pooled connection cannot serve one tenant's value to another tenant's request. An answer that only adds application checks, or that only explains the read filtering, has not reached it.",
      "task": "We run one Azure SQL Database for all our customers and every table carries a tenant_id column. Our Node API uses a connection pool:\n\n```js\nawait pool.request().query(`EXEC sp_set_session_context @key = N'tenant_id', @value = ${tenantId}`);\nconst rows = await pool.request().query('SELECT * FROM dbo.orders');\n```\n\nand in the database:\n\n```sql\nCREATE FUNCTION dbo.fn_tenant(@tenant_id int) RETURNS TABLE WITH SCHEMABINDING\nAS RETURN SELECT 1 AS ok WHERE @tenant_id = CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N'tenant_id') AS int);\nGO\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY dbo.tenant_policy\nADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_tenant(tenant_id) ON dbo.orders\nWITH (STATE = ON);\n```\n\nIt demonstrates perfectly and it passed review. But one customer reported seeing another customer's orders once and we have never reproduced it, and our support team keeps finding orders sitting in the table that the owning tenant's application can neither see nor delete. Every tenant connects with the same database user.\n\nTell us what is wrong and exactly what to change.",
      "states": [
        "The read filter alone does not stop a session writing or updating a row that carries another tenant's identifier, which is how the unreachable rows were created.",
        "Refusing a wrong-tenant write means covering the insert case and the update case separately, because covering only one leaves the other silently allowed.",
        "A row written with the wrong tenant identifier disappears from the writer's own view immediately, so the application cannot read it back or delete it afterwards.",
        "Any caller on the connection can overwrite the session value with no special permission, so it is not a boundary against a caller who wants to cross it.",
        "The tenant value has to be established and the query issued within one acquisition of the same pooled connection, or the value can be a previous request's."
      ],
      "refutes": [
        "A filter predicate prevents a session from inserting or updating a row stamped with another tenant's identifier, so no further predicate is needed.",
        "The session value is per request state that the engine clears when the request or the batch ends, so a pooled connection cannot carry one tenant's value into another tenant's query.",
        "Marking the session value read only makes it safe to reuse a pooled connection across tenants.",
        "The table owner, or a member of db_owner, automatically reads through the policy and sees every tenant's rows.",
        "The intermittent cross-tenant read has to be an application bug in how the tenant identifier is derived from the request, because the database side of this is already correct."
      ]
    },
  "posture": ["read", "write", "admin"],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "entra-id-auth",
    "design-azure-sql-schema",
    "t-sql-correctness",
    "rag-on-azure-sql",
    "vector-search-azure-sql",
    "rag-local-with-container",
    "least-privilege-database-roles",
    "permissions-and-privilege-review",
    "multi-tenant-saas-patterns",
    "secure-by-default-checklist"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a table carrying a non-nullable tenant column and rows belonging to at least two tenants",
      "a login and a matching database user granted only CONNECT and the table permissions the application needs, never db_owner",
      "a second tenant-scoped table deliberately left out of the policy, to exercise the sweep",
      "a client that exposes an explicit connection pool size, so the pooling assertion can run concurrently",
      "on the container, a chunk table with a vector column and no vector index"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "every tenant-scoped table carries a filter predicate and block predicates for both AFTER INSERT and AFTER UPDATE, and a generated policy with a filter predicate alone is flagged as unfinished",
      "the predicate function is declared WITH SCHEMABINDING and returns no row when the tenant is unset, with no OR clause admitting an unset or administrative session",
      "a cross-tenant insert and a tenant-move update are both refused with 33504, verified by running them rather than asserted in a comment",
      "the statement setting the tenant and the statement reading run on one acquired connection, and the answer does not set the tenant through one pool call and query through another",
      "the tenant is cleared or the connection is not reused before it returns to the pool, and no answer offers @read_only = 1 as the remedy for pooled reuse",
      "no answer claims the table owner or db_owner reads through the policy, and any bypass discussion names the pairing of ALTER ANY SECURITY POLICY with ALTER on the policy's schema",
      "a sweep over sys.security_predicates and sys.security_policies runs after any migration adding a table, and returns no tenant-scoped table lacking predicates or carrying a disabled policy",
      "the isolation test runs as the application's own database user and has been observed failing with a predicate deliberately removed",
      "no answer proposes creating a vector index on a table carrying a security policy on the container, and any local retrieval rehearsal uses exact distance search with no vector index"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_rls_filter_orders (order_id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, tenant_id int NOT NULL, amount decimal(10,2) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_rls_filter_orders VALUES (1, 1, 100.00), (2, 1, 200.00), (3, 2, 300.00);\nEXEC sys.sp_executesql N'CREATE FUNCTION dbo.probe_rls_filter_fn(@tenant_id int) RETURNS TABLE WITH SCHEMABINDING AS RETURN SELECT 1 AS ok WHERE @tenant_id = CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N''tenant_id'') AS int);';\nGO\nGO\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY dbo.probe_rls_filter_pol\n  ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.probe_rls_filter_fn(tenant_id) ON dbo.probe_rls_filter_orders\n  WITH (STATE = ON);\nEXEC sys.sp_set_session_context @key = N'tenant_id', @value = 1;\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_rls_filter_orders VALUES (9, 2, 900.00);\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS visible_to_its_own_writer FROM dbo.probe_rls_filter_orders WHERE order_id = 9;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The measurement the whole skill turns on, made executable: with a filter predicate alone, a session holding tenant 1 writes a row stamped tenant 2, the statement succeeds with no error, and the row is then invisible to the application that created it. Measured 2026-08-28. Zero here is the leak, not the absence of one."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_rls_block_orders (order_id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, tenant_id int NOT NULL, amount decimal(10,2) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_rls_block_orders VALUES (1, 1, 100.00), (3, 2, 300.00);\nEXEC sys.sp_executesql N'CREATE FUNCTION dbo.probe_rls_block_fn(@tenant_id int) RETURNS TABLE WITH SCHEMABINDING AS RETURN SELECT 1 AS ok WHERE @tenant_id = CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N''tenant_id'') AS int);';\nGO\nGO\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY dbo.probe_rls_block_pol\n  ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.probe_rls_block_fn(tenant_id) ON dbo.probe_rls_block_orders,\n  ADD BLOCK PREDICATE dbo.probe_rls_block_fn(tenant_id) ON dbo.probe_rls_block_orders AFTER INSERT,\n  ADD BLOCK PREDICATE dbo.probe_rls_block_fn(tenant_id) ON dbo.probe_rls_block_orders AFTER UPDATE\n  WITH (STATE = ON);\nEXEC sys.sp_set_session_context @key = N'tenant_id', @value = 1;\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_rls_block_orders VALUES (9, 2, 900.00);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 33504
        },
        "why": "The same cross-tenant insert, refused once the block predicate is in place. Measured 2026-08-28: Msg 33504, the target object has a block predicate that conflicts with this operation. Microsoft Learn states that security policies apply to all users including dbo, so the administrative connection a probe runs on is subject to it too."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_orders (order_id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, tenant_id int NOT NULL, amount decimal(10,2) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_orders VALUES (1, 1, 100.00), (3, 2, 300.00);\nEXEC sys.sp_executesql N'CREATE FUNCTION dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_fn(@tenant_id int) RETURNS TABLE WITH SCHEMABINDING AS RETURN SELECT 1 AS ok WHERE @tenant_id = CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N''tenant_id'') AS int);';\nGO\nGO\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_pol\n  ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_fn(tenant_id) ON dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_orders,\n  ADD BLOCK PREDICATE dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_fn(tenant_id) ON dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_orders AFTER INSERT\n  WITH (STATE = ON);\nEXEC sys.sp_set_session_context @key = N'tenant_id', @value = 1;\nUPDATE dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_orders SET tenant_id = 2 WHERE order_id = 1;\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS still_visible_to_tenant_1 FROM dbo.probe_rls_halfblock_orders WHERE order_id = 1;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "AFTER INSERT alone closes half the hole. Measured 2026-08-28: with AFTER UPDATE dropped, the tenant-move update succeeded with no error and the row left the tenant, which this probe reads back as the row no longer being visible to the session that owned it. The skill names both operations separately because of exactly this run."
      },
      {
        "sql": "EXEC sys.sp_set_session_context @key = N'probe_readonly_key', @value = 1, @read_only = 1;\nEXEC sys.sp_set_session_context @key = N'probe_readonly_key', @value = 2;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 15664
        },
        "why": "The remedy an agent offers for pooled reuse, and the measured reason it is not one: Msg 15664 refuses the next caller's CORRECT attempt to claim its own tenant, while the forgetful path that never sets the context at all sails past unaffected. Measured 2026-08-28. A key name of its own is used so the probe cannot poison another probe's session."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_rls_failopen_orders (order_id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, tenant_id int NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_rls_failopen_orders VALUES (1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 2), (4, 2);\nEXEC sys.sp_executesql N'CREATE FUNCTION dbo.probe_rls_failopen_fn(@tenant_id int) RETURNS TABLE WITH SCHEMABINDING AS RETURN SELECT 1 AS ok WHERE @tenant_id = CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N''tenant_id'') AS int) OR SESSION_CONTEXT(N''tenant_id'') IS NULL;';\nGO\nGO\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY dbo.probe_rls_failopen_pol\n  ADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.probe_rls_failopen_fn(tenant_id) ON dbo.probe_rls_failopen_orders\n  WITH (STATE = ON);\nEXEC sys.sp_set_session_context @key = N'tenant_id', @value = NULL;\nSELECT\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dbo.probe_rls_failopen_orders) AS rows_visible_with_no_tenant,\n  (SELECT CAST(is_enabled AS int) FROM sys.security_policies WHERE name = 'probe_rls_failopen_pol') AS policy_enabled;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              4,
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "A policy that is present, enabled, schema bound, and enforcing nothing, because one OR clause admits an unset session. Measured 2026-08-28: all four rows returned with no tenant set while is_enabled stayed 1. No catalog view tells this apart from a correct policy, which is why the skill demands the predicate be read rather than the policy be listed."
      }
    ]
  },
